  "why": "The statement asks only whether the set of amicable numbers is infinite. Every counting result in the record (Kanold, Erdős, Pomerance 1981/2015, Pollack 2026) is an upper bound on A(x); no upper bound on a counting function can establish infinitude, and none of these can refute it, so they do not move a quantity on the question asked. They settle a different question (how sparse the set is, which is Erdős 830's neighbouring territory, not this bullet), and I scored them variant rather than bound. Pollack 2026 is the close call: it is a genuine, apparently strongest-known upper bound on A(x) whose proof the author says ChatGPT Astra produced (generated, from the abstract's own words), but by the statement's wording it does not bear on infinitude. Nguyen-Pomerance bounds a reciprocal sum (variant). Dimitrov's generalisations and the psi analogue are analogues (variant); the polygon and knot papers share only the word 'amicable' (variant). The Euler translation, the Mavaddat-Alikhani identities, the two Lean formalisations and the AlphaProof Nexus campaign settle or bound nothing here (none). Formal-conjectures dated 2025 by copyright, hence after the snapshot.",
  "doubts": "If one reads the problem as Erdős 830 including its part (ii) on A(x) > x^(1-o(1)), the Pollack bound would bear on that part (an upper bound of shape x^(1-o(1)) is consistent with it and cannot refute it), but it still settles nothing; under a looser reading where any A(x) bound counts as 'bound', Pollack would be AI-generated progress, unreviewed, with only Lean/README support and the author's responsibility statement. I chose variant on the statement's wording.",
